        <p class="lead text-xl text-gray-700 font-medium">
          Fifth episode in the series. The first set out what a management rhythm is, the second cut the dashboard down to the indicators that actually trigger a decision, the third kept a record of what had been settled, the fourth followed a priority on its way down to the front line. What remains is the place where all of this actually happens: the weekly meeting.
        </p>

        <p>
          The figure framing this episode comes from Atlassian's <em>The State of Teams 2026</em>, published on 27 April 2026 and based on 12,035 knowledge workers and 173 large-company executives. <strong>87% of respondents say they lack the time or capacity to coordinate, with everyone absorbed by executing their own workload.</strong> In other words: the one moment in the week when coordination could happen is precisely that meeting.
        </p>

        <h2>You have already tried to shorten it</h2>

        <p>
          No management team lets its weekly meeting drift without trying something. Three approaches show up almost everywhere, and all three are sound instincts. Each of them, however, stops just short of the decision.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The agenda sent the day before</strong>: everyone knows what will be discussed, which is already a great deal. The first item still takes the whole hour. <strong>The fifteen-minute stand-up</strong>: shorter, livelier, everyone speaks — but people leave without knowing who does what. <strong>The minutes circulated the next day</strong>: six paragraphs, read by nobody; the decisions are in there, buried in the rest.
        </p>

        <p>
          What the three have in common fits in one sentence: <strong>they all take care of the beginning of the meeting, while the decision is settled in the last five minutes.</strong> The entrance is carefully prepared, and people leave the room with nothing fixed.
        </p>

        <h2>Where the Monday hour goes</h2>

        <p>
          Nobody is doing their job badly. The hour fills itself, and three mechanisms are enough to absorb it entirely.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The round-the-table update.</strong> Everyone recounts their week, honestly and without repeating themselves. Twenty minutes later, only the past has been discussed — and forty minutes remain for everything still to come.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The item that overruns.</strong> One subject is urgent that morning, it gets handled thoroughly, and it takes the rest of the hour. Taken on its own, that is reasonable. The four other items wait until next week, where a new urgent subject will be waiting for them in turn.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The exit with no name and no date.</strong> This is the decisive mechanism, and the quietest: people part on "let's sync up again". The who and the when stay in everyone's head, and each person keeps a slightly different version. The next day, two participants asked separately give different answers.
        </p>

        <p>
          The effect has a price. An order that three people believed a fourth had placed goes out a fortnight late, and that delay is paid for in expedited-freight charges. The decision had genuinely been taken in the room. It had simply never become a written line with a name beside it.
        </p>

        <h2>Forty-five minutes, four sections</h2>

        <p>
          What follows requires no tool, no software and no extra meeting — which is exactly the point. The weekly meeting fits into four sections, in this order.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>① What moved — 10 minutes.</strong> The week's figures, stated without commentary. No narrative, no justification: facts everyone already has in front of them. Commentary, where it is needed, belongs to the next section.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>② What is stuck — 15 minutes.</strong> Two or three stalled items, and what would unblock them. You name them, you do not work them: working an item in the room is the surest way to spend the whole hour on it. The work happens afterwards, with two or three people.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>③ What gets decided — 15 minutes.</strong> The decisions that cannot wait until next week, taken one at a time through to a yes or a no. A postponed decision is a decision, provided it leaves the room with a date.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>④ What gets written — 5 minutes.</strong> Three lines: the decision, the name of the person who owns it, the date. Read aloud before anyone leaves the room. This is the section that always gets cut, and it is the only one that outlives the meeting.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-600 rounded-r-xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-blue-900 mb-1">Our advice</p>
          <p class="text-blue-900">You hold this meeting every week, and you were right to put it in place: a team that meets every Monday recovers far faster than a team that gathers once a quarter. What is missing lies elsewhere. Keep the last five minutes, even if you skip the first three sections. Three lines written before people leave the room carry further than a completely redesigned agenda.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-teal rounded-r-xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-1">Diagnostic question</p>
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">How many decisions came out of your last weekly meeting in writing? Then ask two participants what was decided on Monday: do you get the same answer?</p>
        </div>

        <h2>Why this matters in New Caledonia</h2>

        <p>
          Activity has picked up in New Caledonia, but the teams carrying it are leaner than they were two years ago. When one person follows three files instead of one, a misunderstanding costs more: there is no slack left to catch a forgotten order or a decision that was understood the wrong way. That is exactly the situation in which five minutes of writing are worth more than an extra meeting.
        </p>

        <p>
          Your Monday meeting probably does not need to be cancelled. It needs an ending — and that is a far shorter piece of work.
        </p>

